        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "Can I realistically manage AEO myself with software, or do I need to hire a specialist agency?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "DIY AEO is realistic if you have someone comfortable with SEO, structured data, and content writing, and you’re willing to manually track snippets and AI mentions. Without that skill set, you may struggle to turn recommendations into effective changes.\n\nEven with software, you still need humans to interpret insights, prioritize pages, and rewrite content into answer-friendly formats. The tools don’t fully replace expertise.\n\nKey factors:\n- In-house ability to implement JSON‑LD and validate schema\n- Copywriting resources to create Q&A and how‑to content\n- Familiarity with Search Console, analytics, and AI platforms\n- Time commitment for ongoing iteration (weekly or monthly)\n\nIf you’re new to SEO, start small: pick a few pages, follow an AEO checklist, and use free tools to validate your changes. If results stall or you’re overwhelmed, that’s a sign to consider outside help.\n\nThink of AEO as a skill you build; software turns that skill into scale, not a substitute for understanding the basics.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "You can manage AEO yourself if someone on your team understands SEO, schema, and content writing and can commit to ongoing iteration. AEO software helps with monitoring and recommendations, but you still need people to implement changes and interpret the data."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "You can manage AEO yourself if someone on your team understands SEO, schema, and content writing and can commit to ongoing iteration. AEO software helps with mo"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 153,
      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/how-is-aeo-software-different-from-traditional-seo-tools-i-already-use",
        "name": "How is AEO software different from traditional SEO tools I already use?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "How is AEO software different from traditional SEO tools I already use?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "AEO software zeroes in on how well your content answers real questions and how often it’s cited in snippets and AI tools, while traditional SEO software focuses more on rankings, backlinks, and technical health. They overlap, but the lens and metrics are different.\n\nIn practice, AEO is like an answer-focused layer on top of SEO, emphasizing structured Q&A, conversational content, and AI/voice visibility rather than just blue links.\n\nKey factors:\n- AEO: snippet coverage, AI citations, FAQ/HowTo schema, question intent\n- SEO: keyword rankings, crawl errors, backlinks, site speed\n- AEO tools often integrate with or complement SEO suites\n- Answer engines increasingly influence discovery and brand recall\n\nYou don’t have to pick one or the other; use SEO tools to keep your site healthy and authoritative, and AEO tools to ensure that authority translates into actual answers people see.\n\nWhen comparing software, look for platforms that bridge both worlds instead of treating AI answers and traditional search as separate silos.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "AEO software focuses on how your content answers real questions and appears in snippets, AI tools, and voice assistants, while traditional SEO tools focus on rankings, backlinks, and technical health. They’re complementary: SEO builds authority; AEO makes that authority visible in modern answer engines."
            }
          }
        ],
