        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "I’ve been burned by SEO agencies before—how should I approach AI-crawlable website work differently this time?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "If you’ve had a bad experience with an SEO agency, focus on transparency and testing when approaching AI-crawlable work. You want providers who show you exactly what they’re changing and how they evaluate AI responses, not just vague score improvements.\n\nAI crawlability projects succeed when they’re measurable and collaborative.\n\n**Key factors:**\n- Clear deliverables: audits, implementation steps, and testing plans\n- Access to before/after examples of pages and AI answers\n- Use of your real customer queries in evaluation, not generic keywords\n- Willingness to work within your existing tech and content constraints\n\nIn markets like Los Angeles or Austin, ask for a phased engagement: start with a small pilot on 10–15 URLs and expand only if you see concrete improvements in AI answers. Make sure you retain ownership of documentation so you’re not locked into a single vendor’s black box.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Approach AI crawlability with clear, testable deliverables: an audit, specific fixes, and before/after AI answer comparisons using your real queries. Start with a small pilot, demand transparency on changes, and keep all documentation so you aren’t dependent on a black‑box vendor again."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Approach AI crawlability with clear, testable deliverables: an audit, specific fixes, and before/after AI answer comparisons using your real queries. Start with"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 250,
      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/how-often-do-i-need-to-revisit-ai-crawlability-once-my-site-is-set-up",
        "name": "How often do I need to revisit AI crawlability once my site is set up?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "How often do I need to revisit AI crawlability once my site is set up?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "You should refresh AI crawlability elements whenever you make significant changes to templates, navigation, or key content—and run a formal audit at least annually for small sites, quarterly for larger brands. AI ecosystems evolve, and your site does too.\n\nThe point is to bake crawlability checks into your normal publishing and release process.\n\n**Key factors:**\n- CMS or design overhauls that alter HTML structure or rendering\n- New product lines, locations, or services that need schema and Q&A content\n- Shifts in AI search features or assistant behavior in your industry\n- Accumulating technical debt (redirect chains, orphan pages, broken links)\n\nSet a lightweight checklist for every major release (rendering, schema validation, sitemaps, robots) and a deeper crawlability audit on a fixed schedule. In growing hubs like Denver and Salt Lake City, this discipline helps you avoid falling behind as competitors adapt faster to AI-driven discovery.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Revisit AI crawlability after any major change to templates, navigation, or key content, and run a formal audit at least annually for small sites and quarterly for larger brands. The goal is to embed machine‑readability checks into your regular release and content processes."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Revisit AI crawlability after any major change to templates, navigation, or key content, and run a formal audit at least annually for small sites and quarterly"
      }
    },
    {
