        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "Will adding per-page JSON-LD schema actually improve my SEO rankings or is it just a cosmetic rich snippet thing?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "A solid per-page JSON-LD setup usually boosts rich result eligibility and click‑through rates rather than raw rankings by itself. You’ll typically see impact in how your listings look (stars, FAQs, breadcrumbs) before you see traffic changes.\n\nStructured data is a clarity signal: it helps Google understand entities, relationships, and page purpose. When that understanding is high and content quality is good, Google is more willing to show enhanced results, which attract more clicks.\n\nKEY FACTORS\n- Correct schema type & required properties filled.\n- Clean, error‑free markup validated in testing tools.\n- Schema content aligned with real on‑page content.\n- Overall site quality, speed, and link profile.\n\nImplement JSON-LD page by page, then watch Search Console’s enhancements and performance reports for impressions, CTR, and rich result coverage. Treat schema as an SEO multiplier, not a standalone ranking hack.\n\nUsing a generator that enforces required fields can reduce errors and speed up seeing those rich result benefits.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Per-page JSON-LD mainly improves *eligibility for rich results* and click‑through rates, not rankings in isolation. When combined with strong content and technical SEO, better structured data can indirectly support rankings by improving relevance signals and engagement."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Per-page JSON-LD mainly improves *eligibility for rich results* and click‑through rates, not rankings in isolation. When combined with strong content and techni"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 255,
      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/how-much-should-i-expect-to-pay-for-proper-per-page-json-ld-schema-generation-an",
        "name": "How much should I expect to pay for proper per-page JSON-LD schema generation and setup for my website?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "How much should I expect to pay for proper per-page JSON-LD schema generation and setup for my website?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "For most small and mid‑sized sites, the initial per‑page JSON-LD implementation lands in the low four figures if you hire a specialist, and much less if you use a tool and do it yourself. Ongoing costs are mainly maintenance as content changes.\n\nPricing depends heavily on how many templates and locations you have, and whether you need custom dev work versus using a schema engine or plugin. Cities like Los Angeles, Austin, and Denver tend to have higher agency rates than purely remote providers.\n\nKEY FACTORS\n- Number of page types (service, blog, location, product, etc.).\n- One‑time setup vs. ongoing monitoring and updates.\n- Manual coding vs. automated generator or plugin.\n- Local agency hourly rates vs. remote or productized services.\n\nAsk vendors for a breakdown: template design, implementation, QA, and ongoing monitoring. Compare that to the cost of DIY using a schema generator and a few hours of your team’s time.\n\nTools that standardize schema per template can dramatically cut both up‑front and long‑term costs.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Expect a one‑time investment from a few hundred dollars (DIY with tools) up to a few thousand (specialist implementation) depending on site size and complexity. Ongoing costs are mostly small maintenance as you add pages or change services."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Expect a one‑time investment from a few hundred dollars (DIY with tools) up to a few thousand (specialist implementation) depending on site size and complexity."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 256,
      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/what-s-the-best-way-to-structure-per-page-json-ld-so-i-m-not-duplicating-the-sam",
        "name": "What’s the best way to structure per-page JSON-LD so I’m not duplicating the same schema all over my site?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "What’s the best way to structure per-page JSON-LD so I’m not duplicating the same schema all over my site?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "The easiest approach is to define global JSON-LD for your Organization/WebSite once, then generate page‑specific JSON-LD based on templates (service, article, location, etc.). That balance gives you consistency and flexibility.\n\nYou do *not* want to paste identical schema on every page; search engines prefer schema that reflects the specific content and intent of each URL. Global data is for the brand; per‑page data is for the page’s purpose.\n\nKEY FACTORS\n- Put Organization/WebSite in a site‑wide template with stable `@id` values.\n- Add WebPage plus a content‑type schema (Article, Service, LocalBusiness, etc.) on each page.\n- Reuse shared IDs (e.g., Organization) via `@id` instead of redefining them.\n- Keep page‑level entities unique (e.g., each Article has its own `@id`).\n\nMap your CMS templates (e.g., blog, service, city pages) to schema blueprints and let a generator fill in page‑specific fields. This avoids redundant work and keeps your data layer clean.\n\nA schema engine that supports template‑level logic makes this pattern much easier to maintain.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Define Organization/WebSite schema once globally, then add page‑specific schema (WebPage + Article/Service/LocalBusiness, etc.) on each URL. Reuse shared entities via `@id` and keep each page’s main entity unique and aligned with its visible content."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Define Organization/WebSite schema once globally, then add page‑specific schema (WebPage + Article/Service/LocalBusiness, etc.) on each URL. Reuse shared entiti"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 257,
      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/how-should-a-per-page-json-ld-generator-handle-shared-entities-like-organization",
        "name": "How should a per-page JSON-LD generator handle shared entities like Organization versus page-specific entities like WebPage or Article?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
