        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "Realistically, how long does it take to generate and clean up my citations if I use AI instead of doing everything by hand?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Most AI citation generators can produce formatted references in seconds, but the time‑consuming part is collecting and checking the source details. Plan for a short burst of AI work and a longer human review.\n\nFor typical student or agency projects in Austin or Denver, AI can help you format a full reference list in under 10–15 minutes if you’re organized. The review cycle is what protects your reputation.\n\nKey factors:\n- Initial AI output usually appears in real time (a few seconds per prompt).\n- Time scales with the number of sources and how messy your notes are.\n- Manual verification adds extra minutes per citation, especially for academic articles.\n- Style changes (APA to MLA) are quick, but still need re‑checking.\n\nPractically, block 30–60 minutes for a medium‑sized project: first generate citations with AI, then spot‑check every item against the original document or database record. For critical deliverables, add time for a second review or peer check.\n\nUsing AI this way keeps you fast without sacrificing accuracy or trust.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "AI can generate formatted citations in seconds, but collecting and verifying source details usually takes far longer. For a typical project, plan 10–15 minutes to generate everything with AI and another 20–45 minutes to manually verify authors, dates, titles, and URLs so you don’t submit inaccurate references."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "AI can generate formatted citations in seconds, but collecting and verifying source details usually takes far longer. For a typical project, plan 10–15 minutes"
      }
    },
