            "name": "Can AI reliably handle different citation styles like APA, MLA, and Chicago, or will it mess up the details and editions?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Yes, you can ask AI to generate APA, MLA, Chicago, and other citation styles, but you must tell it exactly which edition and output type you need. Styles evolve, and generic prompts often default to older or mixed rules.\n\nAI models don’t inherently know your professor’s or publisher’s preferences; they follow your prompt and training data, which may lag behind the latest edition.\n\nKey factors:\n- Always specify style and edition (e.g., “APA 7th,” “MLA 9th”).\n- Distinguish between reference list entries and in‑text citations.\n- Be aware that special cases (AI tools, datasets, preprints) are still evolving.\n- Cross‑check against official manuals or up‑to‑date library guides.\n\nPractically, build a small library of prompts for each style you use often, and test them with known example sources to see if the AI output matches authoritative guidance. Adjust your prompt until the results are consistently correct.\n\nThis puts you in control of the AI, instead of hoping it guesses correctly.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "AI can format citations in APA, MLA, Chicago, and other styles, but only if you clearly specify the exact edition and output you want. Because style rules evolve and models can mix versions, always compare the AI output to an official style guide or current library resource and correct any discrepancies."
            }
