            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "For a limited budget, focus on the high‑impact basics: question‑based content, concise answers, FAQ sections, and clean local profiles with consistent NAP data. These alone can get you into many voice results without expensive tools.\n\nYou don’t need every advanced feature at first—prioritize what directly affects assistants’ ability to find and read your answers. Many CMS platforms now support simple schema plugins, further reducing costs.\n\nKey factors:\n- Optimize 5–10 key pages instead of the whole site\n- Use free tools (Search Console, autocomplete, Q&A tools) to find questions\n- Implement FAQ and LocalBusiness schema via free or low‑cost plugins\n- Ensure mobile speed by fixing obvious performance issues\n\nPractically, treat voice search optimization as “layered” SEO: get fundamentals right, then add sophistication when results justify more spend. Document changes so you can see which tweaks move the needle.\n\nThis approach lets budget‑constrained businesses compete for voice visibility without feeling locked out by enterprise‑level pricing.",
