        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "How important is schema markup really for voice search optimization, and which types should I use?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Schema markup (like FAQ, LocalBusiness, HowTo, Product, and Speakable) helps search engines and assistants understand your content structure and key details, greatly increasing your chances of being used for voice answers. Without it, your content may still rank, but machines have to work harder to interpret it.\n\nSchema doesn’t replace good content—it amplifies it. Think of it as adding labels and context for what’s already on the page.\n\nKey factors:\n- FAQ schema for Q&A sections\n- LocalBusiness schema for address, hours, and contact details\n- Speakable schema for sections suitable for text‑to‑speech\n- Product schema for pricing, availability, and reviews\n\nPractically, use plugins or tag managers if you’re not comfortable with raw code, and validate markup with testing tools. Start with FAQ and LocalBusiness—they’re the highest impact for most businesses.\n\nStrong schema is often the difference between “ranking somewhere” and being chosen as the spoken answer.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Schema markup is critical for voice search because it tells assistants exactly what your content represents. Start with FAQ and LocalBusiness schema, then add Speakable, HowTo, or Product schema where relevant. It doesn’t replace good content but significantly boosts your chances of becoming the spoken answer."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Schema markup is critical for voice search because it tells assistants exactly what your content represents. Start with FAQ and LocalBusiness schema, then add S"
      }
