              "text": "Local businesses in cities like Los Angeles, San Diego, Austin, Denver, and Salt Lake City benefit the most from voice search when their Google Business Profile, NAP data, local reviews, and city‑specific content are tightly aligned. Assistants lean heavily on local signals for “near me” and intent‑based queries.\n\nUrban markets are competitive, so small gaps—like missing hours, weak reviews, or vague location copy—can cost you visibility. Voice amplifies local strengths and exposes local weaknesses.\n\nKey factors:\n- Complete, optimized Google Business Profile with photos and categories\n- Consistent name, address, phone across directories\n- Location pages mentioning neighborhoods and landmarks\n- Active review generation and responses\n\nPractically, audit your local presence first, then layer question‑based content and schema on those same pages. Ask: “If someone in Austin asked their phone for this service near them, would my listing look obviously right and reliable?”\n\nFor multi‑city businesses, a city‑by‑city local voice strategy is essential rather than one generic approach.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "In those cities, voice search relies heavily on strong local SEO: a complete Google Business Profile, consistent NAP data, good reviews, and city‑specific content that mentions neighborhoods and landmarks. Pair that with question‑based content and FAQ schema to win “near me” and assistant‑driven queries."
