        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "How can I tell if my voice search optimization is actually working and not just changing things on paper?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "You’ll know your voice search optimization is working when you start winning more featured snippets, see growth in long, conversational queries, and notice rises in calls, directions requests, and branded searches—especially from mobile users in cities like Denver or San Diego. These are practical proxies for voice impact.\n\nDirect “voice only” metrics are limited, so you rely on related indicators rather than a single dashboard number.\n\nKey factors:\n- Increased snippets and “People Also Ask” visibility for key questions\n- More impressions/clicks on longer, question‑style queries\n- Growth in local actions (calls, directions, website clicks from profiles)\n- Qualitative feedback (“I found you through my phone/assistant”) \n\nPractically, set a baseline before changes, then monitor monthly. Tag key answer pages and watch their query mix evolve.\n\nThis helps you connect the dots between optimization work and tangible business impact, beyond raw rankings.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Track wins in featured snippets and “People Also Ask,” growth in long, conversational queries, and increases in calls, directions requests, and profile clicks—especially from mobile. Voice metrics aren’t isolated, so these related signals are the best proof your voice search optimization is genuinely paying off."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Track wins in featured snippets and “People Also Ask,” growth in long, conversational queries, and increases in calls, directions requests, and profile clicks—e"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 339,
      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/what-are-the-biggest-mistakes-people-make-with-voice-search-optimization-that-i",
        "name": "What are the biggest mistakes people make with voice search optimization that I should avoid?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "What are the biggest mistakes people make with voice search optimization that I should avoid?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "The biggest mistake is treating voice search optimization as keyword stuffing for “near me” phrases instead of rethinking content, answers, and structure. This leads to clumsy copy that neither users nor assistants trust.\n\nOther pitfalls include overusing schema, ignoring mobile speed, and copying FAQs from competitors instead of using your own customer questions.\n\nKey factors:\n- Writing for humans and assistants, not just algorithms\n- Keeping schema accurate and relevant to on‑page content\n- Prioritizing mobile UX and performance\n- Using authentic, brand‑specific FAQs\n\nPractically, avoid shortcuts like generic FAQ dumps or automated schema on every paragraph. Instead, invest in clear, honest answers and validate that markup matches reality.\n\nQuality‑first voice optimization builds durable trust with both users and platforms, rather than chasing temporary ranking tricks.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Avoid treating voice search as keyword stuffing for “near me” phrases, using generic or copied FAQs, over‑or misusing schema, and ignoring mobile speed and UX. Focus instead on clear, human‑friendly answers to real questions, with accurate schema and strong technical performance underpinning your content."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Avoid treating voice search as keyword stuffing for “near me” phrases, using generic or copied FAQs, over‑or misusing schema, and ignoring mobile speed and UX."
      }
    },
