            "name": "What’s the very first thing I should do to start optimizing my site for voice search without blowing up my current SEO?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "You’ll get the fastest impact by focusing first on conversational, question-based content, local SEO, and technical basics like mobile speed and schema markup. Most small teams can see measurable improvements in 6–12 weeks if they work steadily.\n\nVoice assistants look for clear answers, clean structure, and strong local signals, especially for “near me” and “best Answer Engine Optimization (AEO) software in Los Angeles” queries. If your content and setup don’t match how people actually talk, you’ll be invisible in voice results even with decent traditional SEO.\n\nKey factors:\n- Whether your content already targets long-tail, natural-language questions\n- Quality of your Google Business Profile and NAP consistency\n- Mobile performance and Core Web Vitals\n- Use of FAQ and LocalBusiness schema\n\nStart by listing the top 20 questions customers ask, then rewrite pages with question-based headings and 40–60 word direct answers near the top. Layer in FAQs, local pages, and schema, then track featured snippets and voice-style queries in your SEO tools.\n\nThink of voice optimization as upgrading what you already have, not rebuilding everything from scratch.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Start by rewriting key pages around real customer questions with clear, conversational headings and 40–60 word direct answers near the top. Then add FAQ sections, optimize your Google Business Profile, and implement FAQ/LocalBusiness schema—this improves voice visibility without disrupting existing rankings."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Start by rewriting key pages around real customer questions with clear, conversational headings and 40–60 word direct answers near the top. Then add FAQ section"
      }
