          {
            "name": "What’s different about optimizing for voice search in a city like Denver compared to doing generic SEO changes on my site?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "For a local-heavy city like Denver, focus first on your Google Business Profile, local pages, and reviews, then align your site content with the actual voice questions people ask there. This combination gives assistants clear, trusted local answers to surface.\n\nDenver users often ask for “open now,” “near me,” and weather or traffic-related specifics. If your location data, hours, and services aren’t crystal clear, assistants default to better-structured competitors.\n\nKey factors:\n- Verified, complete Google Business Profile with Denver address and hours\n- Location-specific pages answering “best,” “near me,” and “how much” questions\n- Consistent NAP across major local directories\n- Review quantity, recency, and sentiment\n\nWrite Denver-focused FAQ sections that sound like real spoken questions, and embed them on relevant pages with LocalBusiness and FAQ schema. Then test Google Assistant with your own queries to see whether it chooses you, a directory, or a competitor.\n\nTreat Denver voice optimization as advanced local SEO with extra clarity for assistants.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "In Denver, voice search optimization leans heavily on strong local SEO: a complete Google Business Profile, Denver-specific pages, consistent NAP, and reviews. Combine that with conversational FAQs and schema so assistants can confidently pick you for “near me” and “open now” queries."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "In Denver, voice search optimization leans heavily on strong local SEO: a complete Google Business Profile, Denver-specific pages, consistent NAP, and reviews."
      }
