            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "For voice search, prioritize long-tail, question-based, and conversational keywords that directly reflect how people speak about your product or service. Single-word or very short phrases rarely win voice results on their own.\n\nMost voice assistants pull answers from content that matches a complete, natural question, then provides a concise, clearly structured response. Keywords that mirror those questions and answers are far more valuable than generic head terms.\n\nKey factors:\n- Long-tail phrases (5+ words) that embed clear intent\n- Question forms starting with how/what/why/where/when/who\n- Conversational variants (“what’s the easiest way to…”) not just stiff wording\n- Phrases likely to trigger featured snippets or FAQ results\n\nPractically, take your main short keywords and expand each into 5–10 real questions someone would say out loud, then build FAQ blocks and content around those. Monitor what actually brings traffic and refine your list quarterly.\n\nThink of “most important” as “most useful for natural questions,” not just “highest search volume” the way traditional SEO often does.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "For voice search, long-tail, question-based, conversational phrases matter most. Start with your main short keywords, then expand each into realistic spoken questions and answer them clearly in FAQs and content. Those natural, intent-rich phrases usually outperform generic head terms for voice."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "For voice search, long-tail, question-based, conversational phrases matter most. Start with your main short keywords, then expand each into realistic spoken que"
      }
