          {
            "name": "If I want to make my business “voice assistant friendly,” what exactly should I be optimizing first—my website, Google Business Profile, or something else?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "You should focus on optimizing the content and technical SEO on your website, plus key profiles like Google Business Profile and major review platforms. Voice assistants pull from trusted, structured sources, so those are the places to prioritize.\n\nMost assistants don’t read random pages; they rely on well-structured sites and verified listings. If those don’t clearly answer common questions about your services, location, and offerings, you won’t be chosen as the spoken result.\n\nKey factors:\n- Website pages with Q&A-style content and FAQ/HowTo schema\n- A complete, accurate Google Business Profile with categories, hours, and photos\n- Consistent NAP (name, address, phone) across directories\n- Review platforms that show up for your brand and category\n\nPractically, audit your site and main listings for missing questions (\"who,” “what,” “where,” “how much,\" “when open\") and add direct, voice-friendly answers. Then add schema markup and keep listings updated.\n\nA professional voice optimization service can bundle this into a single roadmap so you’re not guessing which pieces matter most.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Prioritize your website and Google Business Profile. Make key pages answer common questions in a Q&A format with FAQ/HowTo schema, and fully complete your Google Business Profile (categories, hours, photos). Voice assistants rely heavily on those structured, trusted sources."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Prioritize your website and Google Business Profile. Make key pages answer common questions in a Q&A format with FAQ/HowTo schema, and fully complete your Googl"
      }
