          {
            "name": "I barely have a marketing budget—what’s the minimum viable way to do voice assistant optimization without wasting money?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "If you’re on a tight budget, focus on a few high-impact changes: adding Q&A content to key pages, implementing basic FAQ schema, and fully optimizing your Google Business Profile. These give you a real chance at voice visibility without large spend.\n\nVoice assistants care most about clear, direct answers and structured, trustworthy data. You can provide both with minimal tools if you’re methodical.\n\nKey factors:\n- Prioritizing your top 5–10 revenue pages for voice-friendly answers\n- Using free schema generators and testing tools\n- Cleaning up local listings and NAP consistency\n- Monitoring results via search console and assistant tests\n\nPractically, block a few hours each week to convert existing content into question-based sections and short, spoken-friendly answers. Then layer schema markup and maintain your local profiles.\n\nIf you later decide to scale, a specialist can build on this foundation instead of starting from scratch.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "On a tight budget, focus on your top pages and Google Business Profile. Add Q&A-style content with concise answers, implement simple FAQ schema, and clean up local listings. These few steps give you realistic voice visibility without major spend."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "On a tight budget, focus on your top pages and Google Business Profile. Add Q&A-style content with concise answers, implement simple FAQ schema, and clean up lo"
