        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "How do I rewrite my existing content so it actually works for voice assistants instead of just traditional web SEO?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Start by collecting real questions from your search data, “People Also Ask,” and customer conversations, then turn them into headings that match how people speak to assistants. Under each heading, write a 40–60 word direct answer followed by optional detail.\n\nVoice assistants favor content that mirrors natural speech and gives concise, self-contained responses. Long, dense paragraphs are rarely chosen.\n\nKey factors:\n- Use “how,” “what,” “where,” “when,” “why,” and “near me” phrasing\n- Keep answers short, clear, and jargon-free\n- Place the direct answer immediately after the question\n- Add FAQ schema to flag the Q&A structure\n\nPractically, pick one high-value topic and build a mini FAQ or Q&A section around it, then test similar questions with assistants to see if your phrasing aligns. Adjust based on what you hear.\n\nContent-focused optimization is where many businesses see early voice wins before touching deeper technical work.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Turn key topics into natural-language questions and follow each with a 40–60 word direct answer, then optional detail. Use “how/what/where/near me” phrasing, keep answers concise and jargon-free, and add FAQ schema so assistants recognize the Q&A structure."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Turn key topics into natural-language questions and follow each with a 40–60 word direct answer, then optional detail. Use “how/what/where/near me” phrasing, ke"
      }
    },
