              "text": "In cities like Seattle, Portland, and San Jose, you should lean even harder into mobile and hands-free use cases: commuters, smart speakers at home, and “near me” searches tied to tech-savvy neighborhoods. That means optimizing both for informational queries and quick local actions.\n\nThese markets often have higher adoption of assistants, so competition for spoken results can be fierce—but also more rewarding.\n\nKey factors:\n- LocalBusiness schema and event or service details where relevant\n- Content tailored to scenarios (commute, lunch break, late-night search)\n- Clear calls to action that assistants can support (call, navigate, order)\n- Monitoring smart speaker and mobile behavior patterns\n\nPractically, listen to how your actual customers talk about you or your category, then build city-specific FAQs and actions around those phrases. Use assistants yourself in those cities if possible.\n\nA specialist with experience in assistant-heavy markets can help prioritize which use cases to target first.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Yes. In assistant-heavy cities, focus on mobile and hands-free scenarios like commuting and “near me” searches, with strong LocalBusiness schema, scenario-based FAQs, and clear actions (call, navigate, order) so assistants can quickly connect users to you."
