        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "I don’t have a big budget—what are the highest‑impact Alexa optimization steps I can take myself?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "If you’re on a tight budget, focus on the actions that directly improve how Alexa reads your existing content rather than building custom skills or complex integrations. You can make meaningful progress with disciplined copy, FAQ, and schema work.\n\nThe goal is to align your titles, bullets, descriptions, and FAQs with the natural questions customers actually speak, then test those queries with Alexa to confirm improvements.\n\nKey factors:\n- Prioritize your top 10–20 products or most profitable services.\n- Rewrite bullets and descriptions as answers to “how,” “best,” and “good for” questions.\n- Add or update one FAQ page with rich Q&A that mirrors voice queries.\n- Use free tools and your own devices to test Alexa prompts regularly.\n\nAllocate a few hours per week for voice-focused content updates and prompt testing. That steady, targeted effort often beats sporadic big projects.\n\nIf needed, you can later bring in a specialist just to review your DIY work and suggest higher‑impact optimizations.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "With limited budget, concentrate on rewriting titles, bullets, and FAQs in conversational Q&A form for your top products, add a simple FAQ page, and test real spoken prompts on Alexa. Consistent, focused content changes around how people talk are more impactful than expensive custom skills."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "With limited budget, concentrate on rewriting titles, bullets, and FAQs in conversational Q&A form for your top products, add a simple FAQ page, and test real s"
      }
    },
