    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 567,
      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/how-do-you-actually-rank-in-google-assistant-results-instead-of-just-regular-goo",
        "name": "How do you actually rank in Google Assistant results instead of just regular Google search?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "How do you actually rank in Google Assistant results instead of just regular Google search?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "You rank in Google Assistant by winning the answer Google trusts most for the spoken question, usually via featured snippets, FAQ-rich results, and strong local SEO. That means question-focused content, fast mobile pages, and clean schema markup.\n\nGoogle Assistant reads out what Google Search already sees as the best answer, so you’re optimizing for voice results, not a separate “Assistant index.” Focus on the questions your audience actually speaks and structure your answers so they’re easy for Google to extract.\n\nKey factors:\n- Question-based keywords that match natural speech (“how do I…”, “what is…”, “near me”).\n- Direct 40–60 word answers at the top of each Q&A section.\n- FAQ, HowTo, and LocalBusiness schema so Google can parse your content.\n- Fast, mobile-friendly pages and strong E‑E‑A‑T signals.\n\nPractically, start by mapping your top 30–50 real customer questions, rewriting key pages around those questions, and adding FAQ schema to your Q&A sections. Then track which of those pages begin to win featured snippets and voice answers.\n\nDone consistently, this positions your content as the default answer Google Assistant chooses when users ask related questions.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "You rank in Google Assistant by winning the answer Google trusts most for the spoken query—usually featured snippets, FAQ-rich results, and local packs—rather than a separate “Assistant index.” Focus on question-based keywords, concise 40–60 word answers, FAQ/HowTo/LocalBusiness schema, and fast mobile pages to become the answer Assistant reads."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "You rank in Google Assistant by winning the answer Google trusts most for the spoken query—usually featured snippets, FAQ-rich results, and local packs—rather t"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 568,
      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/how-can-my-business-show-up-when-people-ask-google-assistant-near-me-questions-i",
        "name": "How can my business show up when people ask Google Assistant \"near me\" questions in my city? (Los Angeles / San Diego focus)",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "How can my business show up when people ask Google Assistant \"near me\" questions in my city? (Los Angeles / San Diego focus)",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "To rank for “near me” questions in Google Assistant, you need strong local SEO foundations plus content that mirrors how people actually speak those queries. Assistant pulls mainly from Google Business Profile data and nearby local-optimized pages.\n\nWhen someone in Los Angeles asks, “Hey Google, best SEO agency near me,” Assistant chooses businesses with complete profiles, relevant categories, strong reviews, and consistent local signals. Your website still matters, but the profile is often primary.\n\nKey factors:\n- Fully built-out Google Business Profile (categories, description, photos, hours).\n- Lots of recent, high-quality reviews mentioning your services and city.\n- LocalBusiness schema and NAP consistency across major directories.\n- Location pages using conversational “near me” and neighborhood language.\n\nPractically, audit your profile and top local citations, update descriptions with natural phrases people say, and build city or neighborhood landing pages that answer “where,” “when,” and “how much” questions. For Los Angeles or San Diego, include local landmarks and areas users actually mention.\n\nThis combination makes you the obvious choice when Google Assistant needs a nearby answer it can trust.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "To rank for “near me” queries in Google Assistant, focus on local SEO: fully optimize your Google Business Profile, earn strong recent reviews, use consistent NAP data and LocalBusiness schema, and create city-focused pages with conversational “near me” language. Assistant largely reads from these local signals when choosing nearby answers."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "To rank for “near me” queries in Google Assistant, focus on local SEO: fully optimize your Google Business Profile, earn strong recent reviews, use consistent N"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 569,
      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/do-i-need-special-keywords-to-rank-in-google-assistant-or-are-normal-seo-keyword",
        "name": "Do I need special keywords to rank in Google Assistant, or are normal SEO keywords enough?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "Do I need special keywords to rank in Google Assistant, or are normal SEO keywords enough?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "You don’t need separate “Google Assistant keywords,” but you do need to prioritize long-tail, conversational phrases that sound like something someone would say out loud. Think full questions, not short keyword stems.\n\nAssistant typically responds to queries starting with “how,” “what,” “where,” “when,” “why,” or “can,” so aligning your content with those patterns increases your chances of being chosen as the spoken answer.\n\nKey factors:\n- Full-sentence questions as H2/H3s that match natural speech.\n- “5Ws and how” phrasing plus “near me” and “best” modifiers.\n- Answering in simple, clear language at a 6th–8th grade reading level.\n- Using your core entities (services, locations, tools) clearly in answers.\n\nIn practice, pull question data from Search Console, People Also Ask, and customer emails, then rewrite page sections to use those exact questions as headings. Answer them directly in the first paragraph, then add supporting detail underneath.\n\nThis approach turns your pages into question-and-answer hubs tailored to how real people talk to Google Assistant.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "You don’t need unique “Google Assistant keywords,” but you do need conversational, long‑tail phrases that match spoken queries. Use full-sentence questions as headings (how, what, where, when, why, can) and answer them clearly in natural language. Those question-based keywords help your content become the answer Assistant reads."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "You don’t need unique “Google Assistant keywords,” but you do need conversational, long‑tail phrases that match spoken queries. Use full-sentence questions as h"
      }
