            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Yes, **featured snippets** are one of the strongest ranking factors for voice search because assistants often read the snippet answer aloud. If you’re not competing for snippets, you’re missing a major voice opportunity.\n\nGoogle typically pulls voice responses from the same content it uses for answer boxes and “People Also Ask,” favoring pages with concise, well-structured answers.\n\nKey factors:\n- Clear question-based headings (H2/H3) matching natural queries.\n- 40–60 word direct answers immediately under each question.\n- Simple language, minimal jargon, fifth-grade readability.\n- Supporting detail below the short answer for depth.\n\nReview your top pages and reformat key sections: turn common queries into headings, then place a tight, self-contained answer paragraph right under each. This makes your content “snippet ready” and more eligible for voice responses.\n\nIf you don’t know where to start, build one strong FAQ page and follow this Q&A layout as a template across your site.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Featured snippets are critical for voice search: assistants frequently read snippet content aloud as the answer. To compete, structure pages with question-based headings and 40–60 word direct answers underneath, written in simple, conversational language so they’re easy for search engines to extract as snippets."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Featured snippets are critical for voice search: assistants frequently read snippet content aloud as the answer. To compete, structure pages with question-based"
      }
