              "text": "Spoken search can struggle with names, slang, and accents, but you can improve results by spelling tricky words aloud or adding context.\nFor example, say “Show reviews for restaurant spelled N-O-B-U in downtown Denver.”\n\n**Key factors:**\n- Rarity of the word or brand name\n- Accent and pronunciation differences\n- System training data for local terminology\n- Willingness to spell or rephrase\n\nWhen assistants routinely mishear a particular phrase, create a shortcut or voice command if your device supports it.\nIn cities like Los Angeles or San Diego with diverse accents and languages, speak a bit slower and avoid overlapping speech to give the system a clean signal.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "When spoken search mishears you, slow down, reduce noise, and add context or spell difficult names aloud. You can also create shortcuts for common commands and, in multilingual or accent-heavy environments, keep queries short and clearly enunciated."
            }
