            "name": "Is it safe to rely on spoken search queries in urgent or emergency situations?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "For urgent situations—like finding the nearest ER in Austin or reporting a hazard in Denver—spoken search can be useful but shouldn’t replace emergency numbers.\nVoice assistants can mishear critical details under stress.\n\n**Key factors:**\n- Potential for speech errors while panicked\n- Network dependence; no service means no search\n- Assistant limitations around emergency routing\n- Local emergency infrastructure and response times\n\nIn a crisis, call emergency services directly first; use spoken search as a secondary tool for directions or nearby pharmacies once you’re connected to help.\nPractice simple, unambiguous emergency phrases so you’re less likely to stumble when it matters.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Spoken search can help you quickly find locations and information in urgent situations, but it’s not a substitute for calling emergency services. In real emergencies, dial the local emergency number first and use voice search only as a backup tool."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Spoken search can help you quickly find locations and information in urgent situations, but it’s not a substitute for calling emergency services. In real emerge"
      }
