          {
            "name": "How can I do answer engine optimization on a tight budget without hiring a big agency?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "If you’re on a limited budget, focus AEO on a small set of high-intent questions and use lightweight tools and plugins instead of custom builds. You can get meaningful traction by optimizing a handful of pages rather than your whole site.\n\nBudget constraints mainly affect how much content you can reshape and how deeply you can invest in technical improvements and off-site authority. Prioritization becomes critical.\n\nKey factors:\n- Pick 10–30 questions directly tied to leads or sales\n- Use CMS plugins for schema instead of custom dev work\n- Leverage existing content; avoid starting from scratch\n- Track impact using simple manual checks in AI tools\n\nRank your questions by business value, then concentrate efforts on those with clear revenue impact. Implement FAQ/HowTo schema using free or low-cost plugins and refine answers over time.\n\nEven with limited spend, a focused AEO effort can punch above its weight if you choose the right questions and keep them fresh.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "On a tight budget, focus AEO on 10–30 high-value buyer questions. Rewrite existing content into concise Q&A blocks, use CMS plugins for FAQ/HowTo schema, and manually test in AI tools. Concentrated effort on a few revenue-related topics can drive noticeable gains without heavy spend."
            }
