        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "Does adding schema markup really make a difference for voice search, or is it just another technical SEO chore?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Schema markup doesn’t magically put you at the top of voice results, but it **helps assistants understand and trust your information**, which can improve eligibility for spoken answers. Think of it as structured labeling for your content.\n\nUsing LocalBusiness, FAQPage, and related schema types lets Google and others quickly confirm your name, address, hours, services, and answers to common questions. That reduces ambiguity when choosing a single answer.\n\nKey factors:\n- LocalBusiness schema on your homepage and contact/location pages\n- FAQPage schema on pages that answer customer questions\n- Correct, up-to-date data inside the schema (no contradictions)\n- Validation using standard schema testing tools\n\nPractically, implement basic JSON-LD schema on key pages, reflecting the same data you show users, and keep it updated whenever your info changes. Combine schema with clean profiles and good reviews for best impact.\n\nSchema is a multiplier for solid local SEO, not a replacement for it.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Schema markup helps voice assistants understand your business details and FAQs more reliably, which can make you more eligible for spoken answers. Use LocalBusiness on core pages and FAQPage where you answer questions, but remember schema amplifies good local SEO—it doesn’t replace accurate profiles and strong reviews."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Schema markup helps voice assistants understand your business details and FAQs more reliably, which can make you more eligible for spoken answers. Use LocalBusi"
      }
    },
