            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "If you tried voice SEO before and saw little impact, it’s usually because **the work was too shallow or not maintained**, not because voice search “doesn’t work.” One-time tweaks rarely stand up to ongoing competitor improvements.\n\nQuick fixes like adding “near me” to a few pages or updating categories without improving content and reviews won’t change which business assistants trust most. Voice results favor steady, consistent signals.\n\nKey factors:\n- Whether changes were comprehensive (profiles, content, reviews, speed)\n- Ongoing updates to hours, photos, posts and Q&A\n- Review growth versus competitors over time\n- Monitoring and responding to new customer questions\n\nPractically, revisit voice search with a more holistic, long-term approach: align all your listings, build robust FAQ content, improve mobile speed, and create a simple review pipeline. Review progress quarterly, not weekly.\n\nTreat previous failure as a sign the strategy was incomplete, not that the channel is useless.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Past voice SEO efforts often fail because they were shallow or one-off—maybe a few keyword tweaks without fixing profiles, content, reviews and speed, or no ongoing updates. A more complete, maintained approach that aligns all your listings and builds real authority is far more likely to produce results."
