            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "AI voice and text assistants now work together, so you should optimize for both at the same time. The core tactics—clear Q&A content, schema markup, and strong local data—benefit text answers and spoken results equally.\n\nTrying to separate them usually leads to duplicated effort and inconsistent messaging. A single, conversational content strategy is more efficient and more aligned with how assistants actually operate.\n\nKey factors:\n- Use full-sentence, question-style headings that work for both voice and text\n- Place a 40–60 word direct answer at the top of each section\n- Implement FAQPage, Speakable, and relevant schema types across key pages\n- Ensure pages are fast, mobile-friendly, and easy to parse\n\nThink of your site as an answer library that generative tools can quote in any format. Build it once the right way, then let assistants decide whether to speak, show, or summarize your content.\n\nYou’re designing for conversations, not channels, which makes your efforts more durable.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "You can and should optimize for AI voice and text answers together. Use conversational, question-based content with short direct answers, add FAQ and Speakable schema, and keep local data clean. These shared tactics help assistants both speak and display your brand in their responses."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "You can and should optimize for AI voice and text answers together. Use conversational, question-based content with short direct answers, add FAQ and Speakable"
      }
