              "text": "In cities like Denver or Salt Lake City, Google Assistant’s local results are usually accurate, but they depend heavily on your location permissions and language settings. If it’s suggesting places in the wrong area, it’s almost always a configuration issue.\n\nThe assistant uses GPS, Wi‑Fi, and cell data to estimate location and combines that with your Google Maps data. If any of those are disabled or set to another region, results can look “off.”\n\nKey factors:\n- Turn on **precise location** for your phone and Google app.\n- Verify your **home city** and region in Maps and Assistant settings.\n- Check VPN or spoofing apps that might fake your location.\n- Keep local business data correct by using updated Google Maps info.\n\nGo to Settings → Location and enable high‑accuracy, then open Google Maps and set your home/work in your actual city (Denver, Salt Lake City, etc.). In the Google app, confirm your region and language match where you live.\n\nOnce your location signal is clean, local voice recommendations become much more trustworthy.",
              "@type": "Answer",
