        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "Why is Google Assistant so slow compared to old Google Now, and is there any way to speed up responses?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "If Google Assistant feels slower than old Google Now, latency usually comes from your network, device load, or server side—not something you can fix with a single toggle. You can, however, reduce delays with a few optimizations.\n\nVoice queries must be recorded, sent to Google, processed, and returned. Weak Wi‑Fi, congested mobile data, or heavy background apps add seconds to that path.\n\nKey factors:\n- Use **strong Wi‑Fi** or stable LTE/5G whenever possible.\n- Close heavy background apps that compete for CPU and bandwidth.\n- Keep the Google app and Android system **updated**.\n- Avoid VPNs or data‑saving proxies that slow traffic.\n\nTest Assistant on both Wi‑Fi and mobile data; if one is much faster, that’s your bottleneck. Restart your phone, clear Google app cache, and keep storage from filling completely.\n\nThink of this less as “Google Now was faster” and more as “my environment changed”—once the pipeline is healthy, responses are typically quick.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Assistant speed mostly depends on **network quality, device load, and updates**, not a single setting. Use strong Wi‑Fi or stable LTE/5G, close heavy background apps, keep the Google app and Android updated, and avoid VPNs or data‑saving tools that add latency."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Assistant speed mostly depends on **network quality, device load, and updates**, not a single setting. Use strong Wi‑Fi or stable LTE/5G, close heavy background"
      }
