            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "When results from your AEO strategy are slower than expected, the best move is to tighten your focus on a smaller set of critical questions, improve answer quality, and check for technical or schema issues. Broad, shallow coverage often delays meaningful impact.\n\nIt’s better to be the definitive answer for fewer queries than a mediocre source for many.\n\nKey adjustments:\n- Narrow your target question list for the next 90 days\n- Upgrade answer clarity, structure, and evidence on those pages\n- Validate schema implementation and fix any warnings or errors\n- Add fresh examples, data, or case snippets to show real‑world experience\n\nPractically, treat the slowdown as feedback and iterate instead of abandoning the strategy.\n\nMany strong AEO programs hit a plateau before compounding, especially in competitive niches.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "If AEO progress stalls, narrow your focus to a smaller set of high‑value questions, strengthen the clarity and evidence in your answers, and fix any schema or technical issues. Deepen quality before expanding breadth. Most programs see a plateau before results compound, especially in competitive spaces."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "If AEO progress stalls, narrow your focus to a smaller set of high‑value questions, strengthen the clarity and evidence in your answers, and fix any schema or t"
      }
