            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "You should consider hiring an AEO specialist or using dedicated software when you have many products, complex messaging, or multiple cities like Los Angeles, Austin, and Denver to align.\n\nAt that scale, manually tracking questions, schema, and AI citations gets unwieldy.\n\nKey factors:\n- Number of core topics and locations you need answer engines to understand.\n- Internal bandwidth for audits, content rewrites, and technical implementation.\n- Need for ongoing monitoring of AI summaries and citation patterns.\n- Desire to coordinate AEO with SEO, content, and product marketing.\n\nIf your team struggles to keep identity, schema, and question maps synchronized across dozens of pages, that’s a strong signal external help or tooling will save time and reduce errors.\nLook for support that emphasizes measurement and iteration, not one‑time fixes.\n\nBringing in focused AEO expertise turns answer‑engine optimization from guesswork into a manageable program.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "If you have lots of pages, products, or locations and limited bandwidth to manage audits, schema, and AI monitoring, it’s time to consider an AEO specialist or software. At that scale, structured tooling and expertise help keep your identity and question mapping consistent and effective."
