        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "How is answer engine optimization different from regular SEO, and what are practical examples of that difference?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Answer engine optimization is different from traditional SEO because it’s built around questions and direct answers, not just keywords and backlinks. In practice, AEO examples look like content designed to be quoted verbatim by AI assistants and answer boxes.\n\nSEO pages often target broad phrases like “project management software,” while AEO targets the exact conversational questions people ask and structures content so machines can easily pull a 1–2 sentence answer. Both matter, but they’re optimized for different outcomes.\n\nKey factors:\n- AEO uses explicit question headings and concise answer paragraphs; SEO can be more narrative.\n- AEO leans heavily on structured data (FAQ, HowTo, Product, LocalBusiness); SEO focuses more on metadata and internal links.\n- AEO success is measured by citations in AI chat and overviews, not just clicks from blue links.\n- AEO emphasizes E‑E‑A‑T and unique insights that LLMs can’t get elsewhere.\n\nWhen planning content, keep doing SEO basics but add AEO layers: write for the snippet, schema markup, and conversational queries, especially around support and “how do I” questions.\n\nIf you already invest in SEO, AEO is usually a complementary upgrade, not a replacement.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Answer engine optimization focuses on explicit questions and short, quotable answers, often using FAQ and HowTo schema, while traditional SEO focuses on broader keywords, rankings, and traffic. AEO examples include Q&A sections and help articles engineered for featured snippets and AI overviews, not just organic listings."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Answer engine optimization focuses on explicit questions and short, quotable answers, often using FAQ and HowTo schema, while traditional SEO focuses on broader"
      }
    },
