            "name": "What are good answer engine optimization examples specifically for a B2B SaaS product?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "For a B2B SaaS product, strong answer engine optimization examples include a structured help center, use-case FAQs, and comparison pages written in direct Q&A format. Each should start with the user’s question as a heading and a crisp 40–60 word answer immediately below.\n\nSaaS buyers and users ask very specific operational questions, which makes them ideal for question-led content that AI tools can cite. Turning your support backlog and sales FAQs into AEO content can dramatically increase visibility in AI assistants.\n\nKey factors:\n- Help center articles optimized around “how do I…?” queries, each answering one problem clearly.\n- Use-case pages like “Best project management tool for agencies” that combine scenario detail with a direct recommendation.\n- Comparison pages such as “Tool A vs Tool B” that open with a concise verdict, then nuance.\n- Public documentation that includes plain-language summaries above technical detail.\n\nStart by exporting your top support tickets and sales questions, grouping them by theme, and writing one Q&A-style article per high-value question. Make sure these live on crawlable URLs, not in PDFs or gated docs.\n\nIf your SaaS content is currently generic, an AEO-focused overhaul can make your product much more visible in AI buying journeys.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "For B2B SaaS, strong AEO examples are help center articles built around “how do I…?” questions, Q&A-style FAQs, use-case pages like “best tool for agencies,” and comparison pages that start with a clear 40–60 word verdict. All should be structured for AI assistants to quote directly."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "For B2B SaaS, strong AEO examples are help center articles built around “how do I…?” questions, Q&A-style FAQs, use-case pages like “best tool for agencies,” an"
      }
