          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "How does a help center or knowledge base serve as a good answer engine optimization example?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "A help center built around narrow, real user questions is one of the best answer engine optimization examples. Each article focuses on a single task or problem and opens with a short, direct answer before diving into steps and edge cases.\n\nSupport queries are often exactly what people type into AI tools, so turning them into public Q&A content gives answer engines high-quality material to cite.\n\nKey factors:\n- Articles like “How do I reset my password?” or “Why is my report stuck processing?”\n- Clear question title, 40–60 word answer, then step-by-step resolution.\n- Internal links between related articles and back to key product pages.\n- Structured data and consistent formatting across the help center.\n\nExport your top support questions, deduplicate them, and write one concise article per issue. Make sure the help center lives on crawlable URLs and is linked from your main site navigation.\n\nIf your support knowledge base is locked away or poorly structured, turning it into AEO-ready content can dramatically improve your presence in AI support journeys.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "AEO-optimized help centers use one-question-per-article, clear titles, a 40–60 word direct answer at the top, and step-by-step instructions below. Support queries mirror what users ask AI tools, so converting them into structured, public FAQ content is a powerful answer engine optimization example."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "AEO-optimized help centers use one-question-per-article, clear titles, a 40–60 word direct answer at the top, and step-by-step instructions below. Support queri"
      }
