              "text": "For multi-location businesses in cities like Austin, Denver, and Salt Lake City, answer engine optimization examples should balance global consistency with local specificity. You can create a shared Q&A framework, then localize answers where conditions, pricing, or processes differ.\n\nAnswer engines need both the overall brand story and the city-level nuance to recommend the right location.\n\nKey factors:\n- Core FAQs consistent across locations (what you do, how it works, guarantees).\n- City-specific Q&As on pricing, timing, local regulations, and service areas.\n- Separate, well-structured landing pages for each city with LocalBusiness schema.\n- Internal links that connect global and local answers logically.\n\nDraft a master FAQ set, then meet with each city’s team to adjust answers based on local realities. Implement this structure across your site with clear city navigation.\n\nIf you do this well, AI tools are more likely to recommend the right location for each user’s query instead of treating your brand as a single, generic entity.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Use a shared FAQ framework for brand-wide questions, then create city-specific Q&As for pricing, timing, local rules, and service areas. Give each location its own structured landing page with LocalBusiness schema. This makes your multi-location AEO examples clear and useful to AI tools."
            }
          }
        ],
