              "text": "You don’t have to choose between DIY and hiring help; you can start with simple answer engine optimization examples yourself, then bring in a specialist for scaling or technical depth. Early wins often come from better Q&A structure and clarity, which non-experts can handle.\n\nProfessional support becomes more valuable when you expand into schema, large help centers, and performance tracking across multiple answer engines.\n\nKey factors:\n- DIY: rewriting intros as direct answers, adding Q&A headings, cleaning up FAQs.\n- Specialist: schema strategy, cross-channel AEO audits, competitor analysis.\n- Internal: mining support, sales, and search logs for real questions.\n- Ongoing: monitoring AI citations and adjusting content as models evolve.\n\nStart by implementing basic Q&A patterns on a limited set of pages, then evaluate whether you need external expertise for technical and strategic layers.\n\nIf your team is at capacity, partnering on AEO can accelerate results while you keep owning the subject-matter knowledge.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Your team can implement basic AEO examples—clear Q&A structures, concise answers, and better FAQs—without a specialist. External help becomes more valuable for schema strategy, multi-engine audits, and scaling across many pages. Starting in-house, then upgrading selectively, is usually effective."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Your team can implement basic AEO examples—clear Q&A structures, concise answers, and better FAQs—without a specialist. External help becomes more valuable for"
      }
