            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "You don’t need a separate checklist for every city, but you should add local-specific items for markets you care about, like Los Angeles, San Diego, Austin, Denver, or Salt Lake City.\n\nThese local elements help answer engines associate your brand with geographic intent and surface your content for “near me” and city-based questions.\n\nKey factors:\n- Implement LocalBusiness schema with accurate NAP (Name, Address, Phone)\n- Create city-focused pages with question-first headings (e.g., \"How does X work in Denver?\")\n- Ensure your Google Business Profile and key reviews match on-name and location\n- Use internal links to connect service content with local pages\n\nAdd a local mini-section to your core checklist and apply it wherever geography matters. This way, you keep one master framework but have dedicated steps for location authority in each target city. It’s more sustainable than maintaining five different checklists.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "You don’t need separate AEO checklists per city. Use one master checklist with an added local section: LocalBusiness schema, city-specific pages with question-first headings, consistent NAP and reviews, and strong internal links. Apply those local steps to each target market."
