        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "Does GEO strategy only apply to my website, or do I need to worry about third‑party sites too?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "When your GEO work focuses only on your own site, you risk missing a major part of how AI engines build answers—by citing trusted third‑party sources, reviews, and community content. You need visibility both on your domain and in the broader ecosystem.\n\nGEO strategy should therefore include intentional brand presence on the sites and platforms AI tends to favor in your category. That’s how you increase indirect citations.\n\nKey factors:\n- Which external domains AI often cites for your topics\n- Your presence (or absence) on those sites\n- Opportunities for contributions, partnerships, or sponsorships\n- Balance between owned and earned authority\n\nPractically, analyze a sample of AI answers for your key queries, list frequently cited domains, and plan ways to appear on or be referenced by them. In cities like New York or Los Angeles, this might involve local directories, industry communities, or content collaborations.\n\nIf your brand is invisible in the broader ecosystem, GEO improvements on your own site may have limited effect.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "You need both: GEO on your own site and stronger presence on third‑party sites that AI engines already trust. Generative tools often build answers from multiple domains, so appearing on frequently cited platforms in your niche is key to increasing how often your brand is mentioned."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "You need both: GEO on your own site and stronger presence on third‑party sites that AI engines already trust. Generative tools often build answers from multiple"
      }
