      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/what-if-we-ve-done-geo-updates-but-generative-ai-answers-still-don-t-mention-us",
        "name": "What if we’ve done GEO updates but generative AI answers still don’t mention us, or only rarely?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "What if we’ve done GEO updates but generative AI answers still don’t mention us, or only rarely?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "If your results from AI tools feel slow or inconsistent, it usually means your content changes haven’t fully propagated, or your topics are highly competitive. GEO improvements rarely appear instantly; they’re tied to crawl cycles and model updates.\n\nYou need a cadence of review and adjustment rather than expecting one‑time fixes. Watching trends over months is more informative than checking daily.\n\nKey factors:\n- Recency of your content and schema updates\n- How frequently target engines refresh their data\n- Strength of competitors already dominating those prompts\n- Breadth vs. focus of your GEO efforts\n\nPractically, set quarterly checkpoints where you re‑run key prompts in tools like ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Gemini, compare mentions and accuracy, and adjust your content or authority work as needed. Teams in Austin or Seattle often treat GEO as an ongoing optimization program, not a campaign.\n\nIf you still see no change after several cycles, reconsider whether you’re targeting the right questions for your current authority level.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "GEO impact can lag because AI engines need time to recrawl, retrain, and reassess trust. If mentions stay low, review whether you chose overly competitive topics, updated enough content, or added sufficient authority signals, then refine your strategy over several quarterly cycles."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "GEO impact can lag because AI engines need time to recrawl, retrain, and reassess trust. If mentions stay low, review whether you chose overly competitive topic"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 1160,
      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/how-do-i-choose-the-right-geo-strategy-vendor-or-software-without-getting-burned",
        "name": "How do I choose the right GEO strategy vendor or software without getting burned by hype?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "How do I choose the right GEO strategy vendor or software without getting burned by hype?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Choosing a GEO vendor or software platform should start with your needs: question discovery, content optimization, measurement, or all three. You want tools and partners that offer transparent methods, clear metrics, and alignment with your existing SEO and content workflows.\n\nAvoid anyone promising “instant AI dominance” without explaining how they measure or achieve it. GEO is still an emerging, experiment‑heavy field.\n\nKey factors:\n- Feature fit (auditing, prompt analysis, dashboards)\n- Integration with analytics and CMS tools\n- Track record, case studies, and methodology transparency\n- Support, training, and adaptability over time\n\nPractically, shortlist a few options, ask for a pilot or proof‑of‑concept focused on one topic cluster, and compare outcomes and collaboration experience. Enterprises in Los Angeles or New York often run multi‑vendor tests before committing.\n\nIf a vendor can’t clearly define success metrics for GEO, that’s a red flag.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Pick GEO vendors or tools based on clear feature fit (question discovery, optimization, measurement), transparent methods, and proof from pilots or case studies. Run a small trial on one topic cluster, compare measurable outcomes, and avoid providers who promise results without concrete metrics."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Pick GEO vendors or tools based on clear feature fit (question discovery, optimization, measurement), transparent methods, and proof from pilots or case studies"
      }
    },
