        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "Can we manage GEO strategy ourselves, or do we really need to hire a specialist or buy a platform?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "You can absolutely start GEO on a DIY basis, especially if you already have SEO and content experience. The key is to focus your effort: build a good question inventory, optimize a limited set of pages, and track AI mentions over time.\n\nProfessional help becomes more useful when your scope, stakes, or internal bandwidth grow beyond what a small team can manage consistently.\n\nKey factors:\n- Internal skills in SEO, content, and analytics\n- Time available for audits, writing, and monitoring\n- Complexity of your site and topics\n- Appetite for experimentation and iteration\n\nPractically, a small team in Salt Lake City or Pittsburgh might adopt DIY GEO for a few services, while a large enterprise in New York may hire specialists for systematic coverage across dozens of topics. Start DIY, and bring in experts where you hit limits.\n\nIf DIY efforts stall, that’s a good moment to seek outside perspective rather than abandon GEO entirely.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "You can run a lean, effective GEO strategy yourself if you have solid SEO and content skills and keep the scope focused. Specialists and platforms become valuable when you need deeper question analysis, large‑scale content changes, or sophisticated AI visibility measurement."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "You can run a lean, effective GEO strategy yourself if you have solid SEO and content skills and keep the scope focused. Specialists and platforms become valuab"
      }
    },
