            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "What does “answer‑first” content really mean in GEO best practices, and how do I write it?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Answer‑first content means you lead each section with a clear, direct response before you explain the details. This makes it easy for generative engines to grab a concise answer while still having context to work with.\n\nAI tools prefer content that “gets to the point” because it matches how users ask questions conversationally. They can then summarize or expand based on that front‑loaded answer.\n\nKey factors:\n- Start each page or section with a 1–2 sentence direct answer.\n- Follow with 2–3 short paragraphs of context and examples.\n- Use bullets and tables to structure supporting details.\n- Keep jargon minimal or define it immediately.\n\nTo apply this, convert your top informational pages into mini FAQs: question in the heading, immediate answer in the first sentences, then deeper explanation. Review outputs in AI tools—if they quote your leading sentences, you’re doing answer‑first right.\n\nOver time, this style improves both AI visibility and human readability without adding fluff.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "In GEO, “answer‑first” means you begin each page or section with a concise, direct answer to the main question, then provide supporting context. Write a 1–2 sentence answer at the top, followed by short, structured paragraphs, bullets, and examples that expand on it."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "In GEO, “answer‑first” means you begin each page or section with a concise, direct answer to the main question, then provide supporting context. Write a 1–2 sen"
      }
    },
