            "name": "What’s the real difference between GEO best practices and traditional SEO, and do I need both?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "GEO and traditional SEO overlap heavily, but they optimize for different consumers: SEO targets search engines ranking pages; GEO targets AI assistants assembling answers. You need both if you care about visibility in search and chat‑based tools.\n\nIn practice, GEO emphasizes structure, schema, and answer‑first formatting more than keyword tactics alone.\n\nKey factors:\n- SEO focuses on rankings; GEO focuses on being cited in AI responses.\n- GEO demands clear Q&A and how‑to formats, plus rich entity context.\n- Many technical best practices (speed, mobile, clean HTML) support both.\n- Keyword stuffing or thin content hurts both SEO and GEO.\n\nUse SEO research to find topics and intent, then apply GEO to make those pages AI‑friendly via questions, answers, and schema. Think of GEO as the “second layer” on top of your SEO foundation.\n\nHandled together, they reinforce each other rather than compete.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "SEO optimizes for search engine rankings, while GEO optimizes for being cited in AI assistant answers. They share many technical fundamentals, but GEO puts more emphasis on answer‑first content, Q&A formats, and schema. If you want visibility in both search and AI tools, you need both."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "SEO optimizes for search engine rankings, while GEO optimizes for being cited in AI assistant answers. They share many technical fundamentals, but GEO puts more"
