            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "If you’re in Austin or Denver, start by auditing how your brand appears in AI answers for your main topics, then build dedicated Q&A content hubs with schema targeting those prompts. Geographic context matters for certain queries, but the core method stays the same.\n\nUse AI tools to test how they describe your business or niche today. Gaps and inaccuracies tell you where generative engines lack good sources.\n\nKey factors:\n- Map out the 30–50 most important questions people in Austin/Denver ask about your services\n- Analyze existing content for answer-first structure and local relevance\n- Create or enhance topical hubs with interlinked Q&A articles\n- Implement FAQPage, HowTo, and LocalBusiness schema on key pages\n\nPractically, treat Austin and Denver as separate micro-markets: craft localized examples, references, and FAQs that match each city’s context, then watch how AI answers adjust over time.\n\nThis location-aware optimization helps you become the go-to reference for your niche in each city’s AI ecosystem.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "For Austin or Denver, begin with an AI visibility audit: see how chatbots currently describe your niche, then build localized Q&A content hubs with FAQ/HowTo/LocalBusiness schema. Focus on the specific questions people in each city ask about your services and structure pages around those prompts."
