        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "Can using AI-generated content backfire when I’m trying to optimize for generative AI search?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Relying heavily on AI-generated text can hurt your generative search visibility if the content is generic, repetitive, or lacks real expertise. AI systems tend to favor sources with clear human insight, original data, and distinctive value.\n\nAutomated content often misses nuance, introduces subtle errors, and blends in with similar pages, making it less attractive to cite.\n\nKey factors:\n- AI-written content should be edited by experts and grounded in your unique knowledge\n- Overuse of boilerplate or spun articles dilutes authority and trust\n- Lack of firsthand experience, examples, or case studies weakens citation-worthiness\n- AI search guidelines increasingly emphasize “people-first” content\n\nPractically, use AI as a drafting or auditing tool, not your primary author. Layer in human commentary, stories, and data that models can’t invent.\n\nThink of generative AI optimization as showcasing your expertise to other AIs, not replacing it with machine-written text.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Yes, over-reliance on AI-generated content can backfire if it’s generic or lacks real expertise. Use AI for drafts and audits, but have humans add unique insight, data, and examples. Generative AI search systems prefer citing authoritative, people-first content over boilerplate machine text."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Yes, over-reliance on AI-generated content can backfire if it’s generic or lacks real expertise. Use AI for drafts and audits, but have humans add unique insigh"
      }
    },
