        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "What should I do if my generative AI search visibility improves at first and then flatlines?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "If your generative AI visibility plateaus, it’s usually time to go deeper on topical authority and content freshness rather than just adding more pages. AI systems favor domains that thoroughly and coherently cover a subject over time.\n\nA flat trajectory often means you’ve hit a ceiling on what your current content and signals can justify.\n\nKey factors:\n- Existing pages may be outdated, thin, or poorly interlinked within topics\n- You might lack advanced resources like comprehensive guides, tools, or templates\n- External mentions and links may not have grown alongside your content\n- Schema and freshness signals may be missing or stale\n\nPractically, upgrade key topics with richer explanations, examples, and updated data; tighten internal linking into clear content hubs; and consider outreach for citations. Refresh dates and schema.\n\nThink of it as moving from “covered” to “dominant” on your core subjects so AI has no better source to choose.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "When generative visibility flatlines, deepen topical authority instead of just adding more thin pages. Refresh and expand key articles, strengthen internal linking into clear hubs, update data and schema, and pursue more external mentions. The goal is to become the most thorough, up-to-date source on your core subjects."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "When generative visibility flatlines, deepen topical authority instead of just adding more thin pages. Refresh and expand key articles, strengthen internal link"
      }
    },
