            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "You don’t need any special permission to be cited by AI, but you do need content that’s crawlable, indexable, and clearly associated with a real entity and topic.\n\nAI assistants mostly rely on web content and trusted data sources; if your site blocks bots or lacks basic SEO, you’ll be effectively ineligible. Low-quality, thin, or misleading content also tends to be filtered out.\n\nKey factors:\n- Your site must be accessible to major crawlers (no blanket bot blocks, sensible robots.txt settings).\n- Pages should follow basic SEO best practices: unique titles, headings, meta descriptions, and internal links.\n- You should demonstrate topical expertise through multiple quality pages, data, and references.\n- Trust indicators (author bios, reviews, citations) increase the chance of being used as a source.\n\nIf you’re unsure, run a technical SEO audit and a small AI citation audit; fix crawl/index issues first, then focus on content quality, structure, and authority.\nBeing “eligible” is mostly about meeting a minimum bar of quality and discoverability, not joining a formal program.",
