            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "The main risks of “over‑optimizing” for AI Overviews are building shallow, answer‑only content, ignoring traditional SEO, or trying to game schema and facts—each of which can hurt trust and long‑term rankings. AI favors genuinely helpful, accurate content.\n\nIf you chase citations by stuffing keywords, faking expertise, or misusing structured data, you risk lower organic rankings and potential manual actions. AI Overviews are an output of quality, not a loophole.\n\nKey factors:\n- Over‑focusing on snippets instead of full user value\n- Misaligned or deceptive schema markup\n- Outdated, unverified, or risky advice\n- Neglecting UX, speed, and broader SEO\n\nUse AI Overview optimization as a lens to improve clarity, structure, and completeness of your content—never as a reason to cut corners. Always match schema to visible content and keep facts current.\n\nIf you treat AI Overviews as a quality standard, the “risks” turn into guardrails that improve your whole site.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Yes. If you chase AI Overviews by creating shallow, snippet‑bait content, misusing schema, or stretching the truth, you can damage both rankings and trust. Use AI Overview optimization to improve clarity and quality, not to game the system."
