        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "Can I do AI visibility monitoring myself without buying software, or do I really need a tool?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "You can start basic AI visibility monitoring yourself with free tools, as long as you’re systematic and consistent. A simple manual process can provide enough insight to guide better content and SEO decisions.\n\nThe key is to focus on real buyer questions, track the same prompts over time, and log findings in a structured way rather than casually “spot-checking” AI results.\n\n**Key factors:**\n- Use major assistants: ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google AI Overviews, Gemini\n- Build a prompt list from your search terms, FAQs, and customer emails\n- Record visibility, citations, and how your brand is described\n- Re-run the same prompt set weekly or monthly to spot trend lines\n\nCreate a spreadsheet with 50–100 prompts and columns for each AI tool, then note whether you appear, who else shows up, and whether answers are accurate. Review this regularly to decide which pages or profiles need updating to earn citations. As results become more complex or valuable, you can layer in automation or a specialized platform later.\n\nFor small teams on a budget, a disciplined DIY monitoring routine is usually enough to catch major visibility gaps before they hurt revenue.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Yes, you can start AI visibility monitoring manually. Build a list of real buyer questions, run them in tools like ChatGPT and Perplexity, log when you appear and how you’re described, and repeat on a set schedule. A spreadsheet-based system is enough for early-stage teams."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Yes, you can start AI visibility monitoring manually. Build a list of real buyer questions, run them in tools like ChatGPT and Perplexity, log when you appear a"
      }
