            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "AI visibility results often lag behind your content updates by weeks or months, depending on how frequently models refresh and which sources they trust. It’s normal for improvements to show up gradually, not instantly.\n\nYou should watch trend lines—visibility rate, citation frequency, and framing quality—rather than expecting overnight changes.\n\n**Key factors:**\n- Model update cycles (closed models refresh less often than live web systems)\n- How quickly your updated pages get crawled and recited by trusted sites\n- Volume and quality of content changes you make\n- Monitoring cadence to catch incremental movements\n\nIf results feel slow, verify that your changes live on crawlable, authoritative pages and that they directly answer the questions you’re tracking. Increase monitoring frequency during major content pushes, but resist the urge to constantly re-run prompts daily if nothing meaningful has changed. Look for steady visibility and citation improvements over 4–12 weeks.\n\nTeams that set realistic expectations about timing are less likely to abandon monitoring just before meaningful gains appear.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Expect AI visibility to improve gradually over several weeks or months as models refresh and crawl updated content. Watch trend metrics—visibility rate and citations—over 4–12 weeks rather than expecting immediate jumps after each change."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Expect AI visibility to improve gradually over several weeks or months as models refresh and crawl updated content. Watch trend metrics—visibility rate and cita"
      }
