            "name": "Do I need a big budget or fancy tools to compete for brand mentions in AI, or can I do this on a shoestring?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "You don’t need a huge budget to start; you can manually track prompts and optimize existing content, then add tools or agencies as complexity grows.\nContext: The main “cost” at the beginning is time—researching prompts, auditing AI answers, and improving your content and data consistency. Paid tools and consultants can speed this up but aren’t strictly required.\nKey factors:\n- Manual tracking (spreadsheets) is free but time-intensive.\n- Specialized AI visibility tools add automation and reporting.\n- Content updates, PR, and reviews may involve internal or external spend.\n- ROI comes from incremental visibility, higher trust, and better recommendation share.\nPractical guidance: On a limited budget, focus on 5–10 priority prompts, optimize one key page per week, and pursue a few targeted third-party mentions (e.g., niche blogs or directories) rather than broad campaigns.\nSoft positioning: Treat budget decisions for AI brand mentions like any growth channel—start scrappy, prove value, then invest where you see clear traction.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "You can start on a shoestring by manually tracking a small set of prompts and improving existing content, schema, and reviews. Tools and agencies help at scale, but early gains mostly come from focused, consistent work on content and third-party mentions."
