        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "Are there privacy or reputation risks if AI tools start mentioning my brand more often, and how do I manage them?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Privacy concerns mainly arise when AI answers surface more about your brand than you intend, based on public data, reviews, or discussions.\nContext: AI tools don’t usually access private internal systems; they synthesize publicly available information. The risk is reputational—unflattering or sensitive details showing up in answers—not direct data breaches.\nKey factors:\n- Public complaints or controversies can be summarized in AI answers.\n- Incomplete privacy policies or vague messaging can be misinterpreted.\n- AI can highlight old information you’d prefer people forgot.\n- Feedback mechanisms allow you to flag harmful inaccuracies in some tools.\nPractical guidance: Regularly audit AI answers for your brand, especially on sensitive topics, and update your public-facing content to clearly address privacy and security. Where answers are wrong or misleading, use official channels to request correction.\nSoft positioning: Treat AI as another public lens on your reputation that needs ongoing monitoring and care.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "The main risk is reputational: AI can surface and summarize public complaints, controversies, or outdated information. Manage this by monitoring AI answers, improving how you explain privacy and security publicly, and correcting harmful inaccuracies through feedback channels."
            }
          }
