            "name": "Why do AI tools mention my brand but describe us in such vague, generic terms instead of highlighting what makes us different?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "When AI answers are vague or generic about your brand, it’s usually a sign your content doesn’t provide clear, specific claims that can be safely quoted or summarized.\nContext: AI systems avoid strong recommendations if available information is thin or ambiguous. They default to broad descriptions rather than detailed benefits or differentiators.\nKey factors:\n- Thin product or service pages lead to bland AI summaries.\n- Lack of concrete data (results, features, use cases) limits detail.\n- Missing FAQs and how‑to content reduces context.\n- Weak third‑party validation makes AI more cautious.\nPractical guidance: Rewrite key pages to include explicit value propositions, use cases, and structured FAQs, then ensure reviews and case studies back up those claims. Over time, AI can draw on this richer content to produce more specific, compelling descriptions.\nSoft positioning: Treat vague AI mentions as feedback that your storytelling and proof need strengthening, not as a fixed limitation of the tools.",
