        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "How important is unique data or original insight for earning LLM citations?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Publishing unique, verifiable information—data, frameworks, or clearly defined concepts—greatly increases your chances of being cited.\n\nLLMs want sources they can safely quote; distinctive material is more valuable than generic summaries of what others already wrote.\n\nKey factors:\n- Original research, benchmarks, or case data\n- Clear definitions or categorizations that fill a gap\n- Practical how‑to sequences with concrete steps and examples\n- Consistent phrasing of your concept across multiple assets\n\nPractically, identify areas where AI answers are vague or conflicting, publish a definitive guide or dataset, and reinforce that explanation in your product docs and community posts.\n\nPositioning: Think of \"ship unique information\" as your citation flywheel—each distinct, reliable insight is a new anchor LLMs can return to repeatedly.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Unique, verifiable information is one of the strongest drivers of LLM citations. When you publish original data, clear definitions, or practical frameworks that fill gaps in existing answers, models are much more likely to treat your content as a canonical source they can safely quote."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Unique, verifiable information is one of the strongest drivers of LLM citations. When you publish original data, clear definitions, or practical frameworks that"
      }
