        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "My AI citations keep changing month to month—is that normal or a sign something is wrong?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "AI citations can change dramatically month to month, so expect 40–60% turnover in some categories. This volatility is normal because AI engines retrain, refresh sources, and adjust ranking logic frequently, so you need ongoing monitoring rather than a one-time audit.\n\nContext: A page cited this month can disappear next month if competitors publish stronger, fresher, or better-structured content. Treat drops as signals, not disasters.\n\nKey factors:\n- High turnover is common in fast-moving topics.\n- Strong competitor content can displace your citations.\n- Technical issues (schema, broken links) can cause loss of visibility.\n- AI platform updates can reshuffle which sites they trust.\n\nPractical guidance: When you see a drop, confirm it across multiple prompts and users, identify which competitors replaced you, and analyze their content structure and depth. Use this to guide specific updates rather than guessing.\n\nSoft positioning: Accepting that AI citations are inherently volatile helps you focus on systematic monitoring and targeted improvements instead of panicking over every change.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Frequent AI citation changes are normal; many brands see 40–60% turnover monthly. Treat drops as a prompt to investigate rather than panic: verify the change across prompts, identify which competitors replaced you, and analyze their content to guide specific improvements that can win citations back."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Frequent AI citation changes are normal; many brands see 40–60% turnover monthly. Treat drops as a prompt to investigate rather than panic: verify the change ac"
      }
    },
