        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "Does LLMO strategy look different for companies based in Los Angeles or San Diego compared to other markets?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "In cities like Los Angeles and San Diego, where tech and creative industries are crowded, LLMO strategy should emphasize distinct positioning, niche use cases, and local proof points. You are competing in AI answers not just nationally, but often against dense local ecosystems.\n\nYou can use local case studies and region-specific content to stand out when prompts include geography.\n\nKey factors:\n- Localized prompts (e.g., “best X for startups in Los Angeles”) and how LLMs answer them.\n- Regional customer stories, quotes, and metrics.\n- Participation in local communities, events, and media that models may crawl.\n- Consistent NAP (name, address, phone) and entity details across platforms.\n\nTest city-specific prompts in major LLMs and see whether you appear at all. Then build or refine pages and offsite mentions that explicitly, but naturally, anchor your brand in the LA/San Diego tech landscape. A strategist familiar with those ecosystems can prioritize the highest-impact regional levers.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "In Los Angeles and San Diego, LLMO strategy needs to account for denser tech and creative competition and more geo-modified prompts. Focus on localized use cases, regional case studies, and strong local mentions so LLMs have reasons to recommend you for city-specific queries."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "In Los Angeles and San Diego, LLMO strategy needs to account for denser tech and creative competition and more geo-modified prompts. Focus on localized use case"
      }
    },
