        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "How should companies in Austin or Denver think about LLMO strategy and local AI visibility?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "If you’re in Austin or Denver, the opportunity is that many local competitors still aren’t optimizing for LLM visibility, especially for region-specific prompts. A focused LLMO strategy can help you “own” emerging AI answer territory before the market catches up.\n\nYou don’t need a huge team, but you do need clarity on local buyer questions and the ecosystems you operate in.\n\nKey factors:\n- Common prompts that mention Austin or Denver explicitly.\n- Presence in local tech media, podcasts, meetups, and community forums.\n- Clear articulation of how you serve regional industries (e.g., energy, outdoor, startups).\n- Alignment of your brand entities across Google, maps, and directories.\n\nStart by mapping 10–20 prompts that mention your city and category together. If you see little or no competition in LLM answers, that’s a signal to invest early in localized content and offsite signals. An LLMO partner with experience in these markets can help you move faster than peers.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "In Austin and Denver, LLMO is a chance to capture local AI visibility early by targeting city-specific prompts, building regional case studies, and showing up in local media and communities. Many competitors aren’t doing this yet, so focused efforts can pay off quickly."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "In Austin and Denver, LLMO is a chance to capture local AI visibility early by targeting city-specific prompts, building regional case studies, and showing up i"
      }
    },
