      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/what-if-we-need-llmo-strategy-results-fast-what-can-we-realistically-accomplish",
        "name": "What if we need LLMO strategy results fast—what can we realistically accomplish in 1–2 months?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "What if we need LLMO strategy results fast—what can we realistically accomplish in 1–2 months?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "If you need results quickly, compress scope rather than compressing reality. You can get early traction in 4–8 weeks by focusing on a small, high-intent prompt set and low-lift changes to existing content.\n\nTrying to overhaul everything at once just spreads your effort too thin and usually disappoints.\n\nKey factors:\n- Identify 10–20 prompts closest to revenue (e.g., “[category] for [use case] pricing”).\n- Update 5–10 key pages with clearer sections, FAQs, and structured data.\n- Secure 2–3 credible third-party mentions you can influence quickly.\n- Set modest expectations: directional improvements, not total dominance.\n\nTime-box a sprint, define what “good” looks like up front, and instrument simple before/after LLM checks. A focused LLMO partner can help you design a realistic fast-track plan without overpromising.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "In 1–2 months you can’t dominate AI answers, but you can move the needle by focusing on 10–20 high-intent prompts, tightening 5–10 key pages, and securing a few strong third-party mentions. Expect early visibility signals, not complete market coverage."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "In 1–2 months you can’t dominate AI answers, but you can move the needle by focusing on 10–20 high-intent prompts, tightening 5–10 key pages, and securing a few"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 1496,
      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/can-we-handle-llmo-strategy-in-house-or-do-we-really-need-an-outside-specialist",
        "name": "Can we handle LLMO strategy in-house or do we really need an outside specialist?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "Can we handle LLMO strategy in-house or do we really need an outside specialist?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "DIY LLMO is possible if you have strong in-house content and product marketing, but it becomes challenging as soon as you add multiple regions, product lines, or complex technical requirements. The decision is about complexity, not intelligence.\n\nMany teams start DIY to learn, then bring in specialists as scope expands.\n\nKey factors:\n- Internal bandwidth for research, content restructuring, and schema.\n- Comfort with prompt testing, analytics, and basic technical implementation.\n- Number of markets, personas, and products you must cover.\n- Appetite for building your own lightweight tools or scripts.\n\nConsider a hybrid model: own the ongoing execution, but use an external strategist to design the framework, train your team, and review plans quarterly. That often delivers most of the benefit while keeping costs and dependency manageable.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "You can handle LLMO in-house for small scopes if you have strong content and product marketing, but complexity rises quickly with multiple products or regions. Many teams get the best results by owning execution internally and using an external specialist for strategy and periodic reviews."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "You can handle LLMO in-house for small scopes if you have strong content and product marketing, but complexity rises quickly with multiple products or regions."
      }
    },
    {
