      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/what-s-the-very-first-thing-i-should-do-to-start-using-question-based-keywords-e",
        "name": "What’s the very first thing I should do to start using question-based keywords effectively?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "What’s the very first thing I should do to start using question-based keywords effectively?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "A solid first step is to catalog all the questions your customers and prospects already ask—through support tickets, sales calls, onboarding, and feedback—then align those with search data to identify question-based keywords. This ensures you’re solving real problems from day one.\n\nOnly after mapping these do you need tools or complex strategies.\n\nKey factors:\n- Centralize input from support, sales, and product teams.\n- Turn raw questions into clean, searchable phrases.\n- Check which of these show up in search query reports.\n- Prioritize by frequency and impact on satisfaction or revenue.\n\nPractically, run a simple workshop: ask internal teams to list the top 20–50 questions they hear, compare them to search data, and choose your first content targets. This can be done in a week without special tools.\n\nStarting from your own customers gives you immediate relevance; external SEO tools then help scale and refine the approach.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Begin by collecting the real questions your customers and prospects already ask through support, sales, and onboarding. Turn those into clean, searchable phrases, cross‑check them with search data, and select the most frequent and impactful questions as your first targets for content and FAQ creation."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Begin by collecting the real questions your customers and prospects already ask through support, sales, and onboarding. Turn those into clean, searchable phrase"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 1726,
      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/can-analyzing-question-based-keywords-help-me-improve-my-product-or-ux-not-just",
        "name": "Can analyzing question-based keywords help me improve my product or UX, not just my content?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "Can analyzing question-based keywords help me improve my product or UX, not just my content?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Question-based keywords can reveal “hidden” problems or objections that users don’t state directly in feedback but do search for online—like “is X worth it” or “why does Y keep failing.” Analyzing these queries can guide product improvements and UX changes.\n\nYou’re effectively eavesdropping on the questions people ask when you’re not in the room.\n\nKey factors:\n- Look for “why” and “problem” questions about your product/category.\n- Identify recurring failure modes, confusion, or unmet expectations.\n- Share insights with product, UX, and support teams regularly.\n- Track whether fixes reduce those questions over time in search data.\n\nIn practice, tag each question keyword with a “product insight” label when it signals a deeper issue, and feed those into your roadmap discussions. Revisit the question later to see if its search frequency or sentiment shifts.\n\nThink of question-based keyword research as a continuous feedback loop, not just an SEO exercise.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Yes. Question-based keywords often expose hidden problems and objections—“why does X fail,” “is Y worth it”—that users search for but may not tell you directly. Share these insights with product and UX teams, then watch whether fixes reduce those problem‑oriented queries over time."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Yes. Question-based keywords often expose hidden problems and objections—“why does X fail,” “is Y worth it”—that users search for but may not tell you directly."
      }
    },
    {
