      "item": {
        "url": "aeoptimizer.com/faq/what-exactly-are-conversational-search-queries-and-how-are-they-different-from-n",
        "name": "What exactly are conversational search queries and how are they different from normal keywords?",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "speakable": {
          "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
          "cssSelector": [
            "h1",
            ".faq-question",
            ".faq-answer",
            "[itemprop=\"acceptedAnswer\"]"
          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "What exactly are conversational search queries and how are they different from normal keywords?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Conversational search queries are natural, sentence‑like questions people ask search engines or voice assistants instead of short keywords (e.g., “What’s the best project management tool for a small team?”). They usually include intent, context, and sometimes constraints like budget or timing.\n\nConversational queries have grown with voice assistants and AI chat, so people expect search to feel like talking to a helpful expert. That means longer questions, follow‑ups, and more detail packed into a single query.\n\nKey factors:\n- Typically start with who/what/when/where/why/how and sound like real speech\n- Include context (device, location, situation) and constraints (price, deadline)\n- Often come through voice search or AI assistants, not just typed search\n- Need direct, answer‑first content to satisfy them\n\nTo work with conversational queries, treat them like customer questions: capture them from logs and tools, then answer clearly and directly on your site. This makes your content more discoverable and more useful.\n\nDone well, conversational query support quietly turns search into a smoother, more human experience without users ever noticing the mechanics behind it.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Conversational search queries are natural, sentence‑like questions people ask search engines or assistants, such as “What’s the best X for Y?” instead of short keywords. They carry clear intent, context, and constraints, and require answer‑first, human‑sounding content rather than traditional keyword lists."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Conversational search queries are natural, sentence‑like questions people ask search engines or assistants, such as “What’s the best X for Y?” instead of short"
      }
    },
