          ]
        },
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "I’ve had a bad SEO experience before—how is zero-click visibility any different?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "If you’ve been burned by an SEO vendor before, approach zero-click visibility with transparent goals, metrics, and proofs of work.\nThe strategy should feel measurable and concrete, not mysterious.\n\nContext: Poor experiences often stem from vague reporting and overemphasis on rankings without business outcomes.\nZero-click work can be framed around visible, verifiable changes and clear KPIs.\n\nKey factors:\n- Defined deliverables: audits, schema, FAQs, profile updates\n- KPIs tied to impressions, calls, and branded search\n- Access to dashboards and change logs\n- Clear explanation of tactics in plain language\n\nPractical guidance: Ask any partner to map work to specific surfaces (snippets, PAA, Maps, AI Overviews) and show before/after examples.\nInsist on regular reviews of how visibility translates into leads or revenue.\n\nSoft positioning: A good zero-click program should feel like a collaborative, data-backed upgrade—not another black-box SEO promise.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Zero-click visibility can be made transparent and measurable: clear audits, schema and FAQ updates, profile changes, and KPIs tied to impressions, calls, and branded searches. You should see specific surfaces improve—snippets, PAA, Maps, AI answers—rather than vague ranking reports."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Zero-click visibility can be made transparent and measurable: clear audits, schema and FAQ updates, profile changes, and KPIs tied to impressions, calls, and br"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
