            "name": "How do AI search engines actually measure brand authority—what signals are they looking at?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "AI search engines judge brand authority using a mix of **entity clarity**, **expertise signals**, and **independent validation**. It’s less about keyword stuffing and more about whether a model can confidently explain who you are and why you’re credible.\n\nThey look across the open web: your site, review platforms, knowledge graphs, press, and structured data. When these sources tell a consistent, proof‑backed story, AI systems treat you as a reliable reference.\n\nKey factors:\n- Clear entity data (Organization schema, consistent name/address, knowledge panel)\n- E‑E‑A‑T signals: expertise, author profiles, experience‑driven content\n- Brand mentions and citations from reputable sites and directories\n- Review volume, recency, and sentiment in trusted platforms\n\nTo improve how AI systems judge you, tighten your positioning, upgrade your About and service pages with concrete proof, and ensure your brand’s profiles and schema align. Then deliberately earn reviews and mentions where your buyers—and AI models—actually look.\n\nSoft positioning: The goal is to become the “safe answer” for specific problems, not just to rank for more keywords.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "AI search engines look at entity clarity, expertise signals, and independent validation. That means consistent schema and profiles, strong About/author pages, proof‑rich content, reviews, and reputable third‑party mentions. When those align, models see your brand as a safe, credible recommendation."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "AI search engines look at entity clarity, expertise signals, and independent validation. That means consistent schema and profiles, strong About/author pages, p"
      }
