              "text": "If you have a limited budget, focus on a small set of high-intent questions, basic FAQPage schema, and cleaning up your local listings. You don’t need full-blown AEO across the entire site to see meaningful voice search gains.\n\nThe trick is prioritization: answer the queries that are most likely to lead directly to visits, calls, or signups.\n\nKey factors:\n- Choose 10–20 core commercial questions (pricing, availability, \"near me\")\n- Build one strong FAQ/Q&A page with short, conversational answers\n- Implement FAQPage and LocalBusiness schema on that page first\n- Ensure Google Business Profile and key directories are consistent\n\nPractically, treat AEO like a pilot: measure changes in snippet ownership, voice queries, and branded “near me” results before expanding. You can add more questions over time as budget allows.\n\nIf you’re resource-constrained, a lightweight AEO tool or template-based engagement can stretch your budget effectively.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "You can start small: focus on 10–20 high-intent questions, build one strong FAQ/Q&A page with concise answers, add FAQPage and LocalBusiness schema, and clean up your local listings. This limited AEO effort can still produce noticeable voice search gains without a big budget."
