            "name": "Does voice search and AEO make sense for e‑commerce or SaaS, or is it only for local businesses?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "For e‑commerce and SaaS, AEO can boost top-of-funnel discovery, product education, and trust by owning the answers to common “which, why, how” questions. It’s less about direct cart clicks from voice and more about becoming the authoritative voice in your niche.\n\nVoice assistants often answer pre‑purchase research queries before buyers ever hit a site.\n\nKey factors:\n- Detailed Q&A around features, comparisons, and use cases\n- HowTo schema for setup, onboarding, and troubleshooting guides\n- FAQPage schema for pricing, integrations, and security questions\n- Consistent product data and documentation across channels\n\nPractically, mine support tickets, sales questions, and search data to find the 50–100 queries prospects ask before buying, then build AEO-optimized guides around them. This positions your brand as the default explainer.\n\nIf you sell into technical or high-consideration markets, AEO often pays off in shorter sales cycles and fewer repetitive questions.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Voice search and AEO help e‑commerce and SaaS by owning the answers to pre‑purchase questions—features, comparisons, setup, and troubleshooting. While local businesses benefit from “near me” queries, product and software brands gain authority and trust by being the default explanation."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Voice search and AEO help e‑commerce and SaaS by owning the answers to pre‑purchase questions—features, comparisons, setup, and troubleshooting. While local bus"
      }
