        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "name": "What makes Alexa decide which products or businesses to recommend or mention first?",
            "@type": "Question",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "text": "Alexa only “recommends” products and businesses when they are clearly relevant, well-described, and supported by structured data and reviews. To become the option Alexa mentions, focus on clarity, completeness, and authentic reviews—not tricks.\n\nIf you sell products on Amazon, Alexa’s shopping recommendations lean heavily on that ecosystem.\n\n**Key factors:**\n- Clear titles that describe what the product or service actually is\n- Filled-in attributes (size, use case, compatibility, etc.)\n- Concise but specific bullet points or descriptions\n- Genuine review depth that mentions key use cases\n\nAudit your Amazon listings or business descriptions for clarity and completeness. Use the same language customers use and avoid keyword stuffing. Encourage detailed reviews that mention how and why people use what you offer.\n\nVoice recommendations are ultimately an extension of good fundamentals: clean data plus real customer feedback.",
              "@type": "Answer",
              "description": "Alexa tends to recommend options that are clearly described, complete, and backed by strong reviews. Clean titles, filled-in attributes, specific descriptions, and genuine review depth give Alexa the confidence to surface your product or business when users ask for something in your category."
            }
          }
        ],
        "description": "Alexa tends to recommend options that are clearly described, complete, and backed by strong reviews. Clean titles, filled-in attributes, specific descriptions,"
      }
    },
